{% block prose %}
<div class="callout">
  <p><strong>The short version:</strong> roughly half of New Zealand businesses still have no website, the figure is far worse for sole traders, and nearly three-quarters of New Zealanders say a website is their main way of engaging with a business. That gap is the opportunity.</p>
</div>

<h2>How many New Zealand businesses have a website?</h2>

<div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Figure</th><th>What it measures</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="wft-val"><strong>53%</strong></td><td>NZ businesses that have a website</td><td>InternetNZ, 2025</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="wft-val"><strong>47%</strong></td><td>NZ businesses with no website at all</td><td>InternetNZ, 2025</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="wft-val"><strong>~25%</strong></td><td>Sole traders that have a website — about one in four</td><td>InternetNZ, 2025</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="wft-val"><strong>61%</strong></td><td>NZ businesses saying a website is their most important digital tool</td><td>InternetNZ, 2025</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<p>The 53% figure has not moved since 2022. Businesses increasingly say a website matters — 61% call it their most important digital tool — but the share that actually has one has stalled.</p>

<p><strong>Why this matters for a tradie:</strong> most trade businesses are sole traders or very small operations, which is the group with the lowest website rate of all. If about three in four sole traders have no website, the tradie who does isn't competing with everyone in their suburb. They're competing with the quarter who bothered.</p>

<h2>What New Zealanders expect from a business website</h2>

<div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Figure</th><th>What it measures</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="wft-val"><strong>~73%</strong></td><td>New Zealanders saying a business's website is the most important tool for engaging with it — "nearly three-quarters"</td><td>InternetNZ, 2025</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="wft-val"><strong>52%</strong></td><td>Consumers who see a website's main usefulness as enabling online sales</td><td>InternetNZ, 2025</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="wft-val"><strong>25%</strong></td><td>Businesses who see their website that way — less than half the consumer figure</td><td>InternetNZ, 2025</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="wft-val"><strong>42%</strong></td><td>Businesses who see their website as being about credibility</td><td>InternetNZ, 2025</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="wft-val"><strong>17%</strong></td><td>Consumers who see a website as being about credibility</td><td>InternetNZ, 2025</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<p>Those last two rows are the interesting ones, and they cut against how most agencies sell websites. Businesses think their website's job is to look credible. Consumers are far more interested in whether it lets them actually do something — find the information, make contact, get the job moving.</p>

<p><strong>Why this matters for a tradie:</strong> a site that's mostly a polished brand statement is built for what you think matters. A site that makes it obvious what you do, where you work and how to reach you is built for what your customer came for.</p>

<h2>.nz domain trust and adoption</h2>

<div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Figure</th><th>What it measures</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="wft-val"><strong>74%</strong></td><td>NZ <strong>businesses</strong> agreeing a .nz domain is more trustworthy than other domains</td><td>InternetNZ, 2025</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="wft-val"><strong>58%</strong></td><td>The same measure in 2020 — trust in .nz has grown substantially</td><td>InternetNZ, 2020</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="wft-val"><strong>70%</strong></td><td>NZ businesses with a website that use a .nz domain</td><td>InternetNZ, 2025</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="wft-val"><strong>67%</strong></td><td>NZ <strong>consumers</strong> agreeing .nz is more trustworthy</td><td>InternetNZ, 2022</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="wft-val"><strong>76%</strong></td><td>Consumers who look for domains ending in .nz when searching online</td><td>InternetNZ, 2022</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="callout">
  <p><strong>A note on a number you'll see misquoted.</strong> The 74% figure measures <em>businesses</em>, not consumers or "Kiwis" generally. We had it wrong on this site until August 2026 and corrected it. If you see a New Zealand web agency claiming "74% of Kiwis trust .nz more than .com", they've made the same mistake — the consumer figure is 67%, from the 2022 research.</p>
</div>

<h2>Sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>InternetNZ, ".nz Consumers and Businesses Research" (2025)</strong> — surveyed 750 businesses and 500 consumers, July–August 2025. <a href="https://internetnz.nz/nz-consumers-and-businesses-research/" rel="noopener">internetnz.nz</a></li>
  <li><strong>InternetNZ, "2022 .nz Consumers and Businesses Research"</strong> — <a href="https://internetnz.nz/dotnz-research/2022-nz-consumers-and-businesses-research/" rel="noopener">internetnz.nz</a></li>
</ul>

<h2>What we deliberately left out</h2>
<p>Stats pages are usually padded with impressive-sounding numbers nobody can trace. A few we considered and dropped:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>"X% of consumers research a business online before making contact."</strong> Widely repeated by New Zealand web agencies, including previously by us. We could not trace it to a New Zealand source, so it's gone.</li>
  <li><strong>"Businesses with a website grow twice as fast."</strong> Same problem. Plausible, widely quoted, no primary source we could verify.</li>
  <li><strong>Global statistics presented as if they were about New Zealand.</strong> Most "small business website statistics" articles are US data. NZ's market is different enough that borrowing those numbers is misleading.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you have a primary source for either of the first two, <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">tell us</a> and we'll add it with proper attribution.</p>

<h2>Using these figures</h2>
<p>You're welcome to quote anything on this page — it's why we sourced it. A link back to this page is appreciated but not required. If you're citing a figure, please cite InternetNZ as the original researcher rather than us; we've only collected and checked their work.</p>
<p>We review this page annually and whenever InternetNZ publishes new research.</p>

<hr />

<h2>What this means if you're a tradie without a website</h2>
